INT. SEWING FACTORY - BREAK ROOM - DAY

A cinder-block room with a Formica table and a vending machine that hums. Fabric dust hangs in the window light.

MAUDE (60s, grey bun, thimble on her finger) sits stirring tea. ROSALIND (40s, supervisor's badge, fraying composure) stands at the door with a manila folder. BUNNY (20s, new hire, bright scarf) perches on the vending machine's edge, swinging her feet.

ROSALIND
Maude, I need to talk to you about Friday.

MAUDE
Then talk, Rosalind. I'm sitting.

ROSALIND
The line was short two hundred pieces. The lot was yours.

MAUDE
Two hundred is nothing. We lost twice that in March.

BUNNY
Maude says the machines were running slow.

ROSALIND
Bunny, I didn't ask you.

BUNNY
I'm only saying what she told me.

MAUDE
She's a good girl, Rosalind. Leave her out of it.

ROSALIND
Maude, they're cutting the shift. Seventeen people. If I can't show a reason, it's all of us.

MAUDE
You want to blame me.

ROSALIND
I want to know the truth.

MAUDE
The truth is I've been on that floor since you were in diapers. The truth is you've forgotten who taught you the seam.

ROSALIND
That isn't fair.

MAUDE
No. It isn't.

BUNNY
Maybe I should go.

ROSALIND
Stay.

MAUDE
Stay, Bunny. Hear how it's done.

ROSALIND
Maude, if you quit, it's the end of the matter. I'll put it in writing. No review.

MAUDE
You'd like that.

ROSALIND
I'd hate it. I'm asking anyway.

Maude sets down her tea. She slides the thimble off her finger and places it on the folder.

MAUDE
There. Now you owe me one.
